nullAccessT -- THE SEVEN ACCESSORS THAT LAWFULLY ANSWER NULL, each driven to its
"nothing there", interpreted. Minted 2026-09-25 with the nullAccessorDeref guard.

opDot's accessor switch answers null for parenT with no parent (a fresh copyOf,
which nobody has adopted), registrY with no registry (the same), nexT on the LAST
term, prioR on the FIRST, and firsT, lasT and firstMembeR on a field with no list.
Before the guard every one of them segfaulted on the line after the switch, which
read product.data without asking whether product existed -- born fb9e4de,
2026-09-08. Each row wants its truth value: a null reads FALSE, so 0 for each
single accessor, over naList's
three members, subscripted: nexT on the LAST term and prioR on the FIRST are the
nulls; nexT on the FIRST and prioR on the LAST are their non-null siblings and
must read 1. ⚠ NOT A WALK WITH A BARE nexT: a bare accessor reads lastREF, so in
the original repro (`knN = knN + 1; if nexT;`) and in f31 it asked about the
COUNTER, not the member -- the crash was the counter having no next entry.
firsT, lasT and firstMembeR each have a non-null sibling on naList, which must read
1. parenT and registrY have none that can read true here -- a parent or registry
holding no data is falsy under truthOf -- so their claim is the run surviving to the
sentinel, and H7 (the guard removed: exit 139) is what makes that claim mean anything.
The sentinel sits after all seven, so a crash on any row takes every later row
with it and the sentinel too.
